//Naive approach

class Solution {
    /*
    Why a single Left-to-Right pass is not enough to solve the problem?
    
    Dry Run: 1-pass Left to Right on ratings = [1, 2, 10, 10, 10, 2, 1]
    
    Initial candies: [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]  // Everyone gets 1 initially
    
    -- Left to Right Pass --
    
    i = 0:
    Left neighbor: ❌ None
    Right neighbor: ratings[0]=1 < ratings[1]=2 ⇒ No candy update
    candies[0] stays 1
    
    i = 1:
    Left neighbor: ratings[1]=2 > ratings[0]=1 ⇒ candies[1] = candies[0] + 1 = 2
    Right neighbor: ratings[1]=2 < ratings[2]=10 ⇒ no change
    candies = [1, 2, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]
    
    i = 2:
    Left neighbor: ratings[2]=10 > ratings[1]=2 ⇒ candies[2] = candies[1] + 1 = 3
    Right neighbor: ratings[2]=10 == ratings[3]=10 ⇒ no update
    candies = [1, 2, 3, 1, 1, 1, 1]
    
    i = 3:
    Left neighbor: ratings[3]=10 == ratings[2]=10 ⇒ no update
    Right neighbor: ratings[3]=10 == ratings[4]=10 ⇒ no update
    candies = [1, 2, 3, 1, 1, 1, 1]
    
    i = 4:
    Left neighbor: ratings[4]=10 == ratings[3]=10 ⇒ no update
    // ⚠️ Bug Source: Trying to assign based on a future neighbor not yet processed
    Right neighbor: ratings[4]=10 > ratings[5]=2 ⇒ 
        candies[4] = candies[5] + 1 = 2
    
    i = 5:
    Left neighbor: ratings[5]=2 < ratings[4]=10 ⇒ no update
    // ⚠️ Bug Source: Trying to assign based on a future neighbor not yet processed
    Right neighbor: ratings[5]=2 > ratings[6]=1 ⇒ 
        candies[5] = candies[6] + 1 = 2
    
    i = 6:
    Left neighbor: ratings[6]=1 < ratings[5]=2 ⇒ no update
    Right neighbor: ❌ None
    candies[6] remains 1
    
    -- After L→R pass: candies = [1, 2, 3, 1, 2, 2, 1]
    
    -- Now check if all local rules are satisfied:
    
    i = 0:
    Right: 1 < 2 ⇒ OK
    No left neighbor
    
    i = 1:
    Left: 2 > 1 → candies[1]=2 > candies[0]=1 ⇒ OK
    Right: 2 < 10 ⇒ OK
    
    i = 2:
    Left: 10 > 2 → candies[2]=3 > candies[1]=2 ⇒ OK
    Right: 10 == 10 ⇒ OK
    
    i = 3:
    Left: 10 == 10 ⇒ OK
    Right: 10 == 10 ⇒ OK
    
    i = 4:
    Left: 10 == 10 ⇒ OK
    Right: ratings[4]=10 > ratings[5]=2, but candies[4]=2 == candies[5]=2 ⇒ ❌ Violates constraint
    - It has **higher rating than its right neighbor**, but equal candies.
    - This happens because the right neighbor was **not yet processed** in left-to-right traversal.
    
    i = 5:
    Left: 2 < 10 ⇒ OK
    Right: 2 > 1 → candies[5]=2 > candies[6]=1 ⇒ OK
    
    i = 6:
    Left: 1 < 2 ⇒ OK
    No right neighbor
    
    🎯 Root Cause:
    This approach fails because it attempts to consider the "next neighbor" (future values) which have not yet been processed.
    In L→R pass, we can only compare to the **left (processed)** neighbor.
    We **can't validate based on the right (future)** neighbor yet.
      ✅ That's why a second **R→L pass** is required to handle right neighbors.
    
    */

    public int candy(int[] ratings) {
        // Step 1: Initialize all children with 1 candy (base requirement)
        int[] candies = new int[ratings.length];
        Arrays.fill(candies, 1);

        // Step 2: Left to Right Pass
        for (int i = 1; i < ratings.length; i++) {
            // If a child has a higher rating than the left neighbor, give them more candies than the left neighbor.
            if (ratings[i] > ratings[i - 1]) {
                candies[i] = candies[i - 1] + 1;
            }
        }

        // Step 3: Right to Left Pass
        int totalCandies = candies[ratings.length - 1]; // start from last child's candies
        for (int i = ratings.length - 2; i >= 0; i--) {
            /*
            When a child has a higher rating than the right neighbor, they should receive more candies than the right neighbor.
            We use Math.max to retain the maximum candy count from the left-to-right pass, ensuring the child gets at least one more candy than the neighbor with the most candies.
            To minimize the total number of candies, we aim to give the child the smallest number of candies that satisfies the condition.
            Therefore, we assign the child the greater of the candies given to the left or right neighbor, plus one.
            */

            if (ratings[i] > ratings[i + 1]) {
                candies[i] = Math.max(candies[i], candies[i + 1] + 1);
            }
            totalCandies += candies[i]; // accumulate the total candies
        }

        return totalCandies;
    }
}


